findings.detail:
VERIFIED (Apple newsroom, March 2026): the M5 Pro has up to a 20-core GPU with a Neural Accelerator in each core, up to 307 GB/s unified memory bandwidth, and hardware mesh shading. Apple claims up to 20% more graphics performance than the M4 Pro. Toji's WebGPU best-practices page warns that WebGPU is not automatically faster than WebGL. A fair comparison needs powerPreference 'high-performance', the preferred canvas format, matching MSAA settings and so on. INFERENCE (my arithmetic): the native backbuffer at DPR 2 is 3456×2234 = 7.72 MP, and one RGBA16F full-screen target is about 62 MB. Writing and reading it once per frame at 60 fps costs about 7.4 GB/s. Twenty such full-resolution passes would use about 150 GB/s, roughly half of the theoretical 307 GB/s, before the browser compositor and texture fetches. Therefore:
- keep the number of full-resolution passes small (roughly ≤8);
- run AO, bloom, fog, volumetrics and distortion at half or quarter resolution;
- pack the G-buffer tightly (RGBA8 albedo, RG8 octahedral normal, RGBA8 material);
- use an R11G11B10-style HDR light buffer where available;
- merge tone mapping, LUT, grain, vignette and sharpening into one final 'uber' pass.
This matches the iOS port's finding that bandwidth was the limit. WebGPU on Apple GPUs is tile-based under the hood, which rewards fewer, fatter passes.

(a) Rebuild in 3D, then bake. This is the gold standard. Model each unit and building in 3D and render albedo, normal, depth, AO, emissive and shadow passes from a fixed RA2 orthographic camera. Proven precedents:
- Age of Empires II DE: every asset modelled in 3D, rendered as 2D sprites at 3 resolutions, 32 directions;
- Dead Cells: 3D models exported per frame with normal maps;
- Pillars of Eternity: Maya backgrounds rendered as final/depth/normal/albedo passes, with deferred dynamic lights on top.
Image-to-3D models can speed up blocking-out (as of May 2026): TRELLIS 2 (MIT, outputs PBR) and Hunyuan3D (strong textures). This route gives the frame-to-frame consistency that animation needs.
(b) AI estimation straight from HD 2D frames:
- Depth Anything 3 (ByteDance Seed, released 2025-11-14; monocular + multi-view depth);
- Marigold Normals v1.1 (released 2025-05-15) and Marigold V2 (diffusion transformer, single-step);
- RGB↔X (albedo/roughness/metallic decomposition, open code).
Caveats (inference): these are trained on photos, not tiny iso sprites. Estimating frame by frame will flicker across animation frames and facings. Output needs per-facing consistency passes, temporal smoothing or estimation on a 3D proxy. Depth comes out relative and needs calibrating to RA2 world units.
(c) Classic 2D tools: Laigter (open-source; normal/parallax/specular/occlusion from sprites) and SpriteIlluminator (algorithmic + hand-painted normal/specular/emissive). An arXiv survey compiles normal-map techniques for pixel art.
Recommended encoding (inference): the camera is fixed, so store view-space normals (no tangent frames). Store height as 'pixels above sprite ground line' so it can be written into the depth buffer.

Recommended core: a thin G-buffer pass writes, for all terrain, SHP sprites and voxels:
- albedo;
- an octahedral-encoded view-space normal;
- roughness, emissive, remap mask and baked AO;
- per-pixel depth, meaning the quad depth offset by the sprite's height map.
Then a light-accumulation pass does the lighting:
- one fullscreen sun + ambient + hemisphere term driven by the map's existing lighting (Ambient/Red/Green/Blue/Ground/Level in MapLighting);
- one additive screen-space quad or circle per dynamic light: muzzle flashes, explosions, tesla coils and arcs, prism beams, burning buildings, headlights at night.
This 'classic 2D deferred' approach works identically on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback (MRT). It costs about one small quad per light and scales to hundreds of short-lived lights. That fits RA2, where most lights last under 0.5 s.
On WebGPU, the alternative is three.js ClusteredLighting (verified: ≤1024 point lights, 32 px tiles, 24 exponential Z slices). Its depth slicing is designed for perspective 3D; with an ortho iso camera, 2D tiles with a few linear slices are enough. DynamicLighting (verified) avoids shader recompiles when light counts change, which suits a small fixed set of forward lights on voxel meshes.
Fidelity rule: keep an exact-retail 'Classic' lighting path (the iOS port found and fixed sign and gamma errors in the palette-domain lighting). HD lighting should start from the same map-lighting numbers so night maps, snow maps and so on keep their original mood. Rim light is a cheap view-space normal term (1 - N·V) tinted by sky colour, and it helps units read against busy HD terrain.
